## Proof

**Interpretation.** A function $g:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is "integrable only on bounded intervals" if it is (Riemann / Lebesgue) integrable on every bounded interval $[a,b]\subset\mathbb{R}$, but is **not** integrable on the whole real line $\mathbb{R}$ — i.e. the improper integral $\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}g(x)\,dx$ diverges (equivalently, $\int_{\mathbb{R}}|g|\,dx=+\infty$, so $g\notin L^{1}(\mathbb{R})$).

**Claim.** Yes, such a function exists.

**Construction.** Take
$$
g(x)=1,\qquad x\in\mathbb{R}.
$$

**Verification of the three required properties.**

1. **Continuity everywhere.** The constant function $g\equiv 1$ is continuous at every point of $\mathbb{R}$.

2. **Integrable on every bounded interval.** For any bounded interval $[a,b]$,
   $$
   \int_{a}^{b} g(x)\,dx=\int_{a}^{b}1\,dx=b-a<\infty.
   $$
   Hence $g$ is Riemann integrable (and Lebesgue integrable) on every bounded interval.

3. **Not integrable on $\mathbb{R}$.** The improper integral over $\mathbb{R}$ is
   $$
   \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}g(x)\,dx=\lim_{R\to+\infty}\int_{0}^{R}1\,dx+\lim_{S\to+\infty}\int_{-S}^{0}1\,dx=\lim_{R\to+\infty}R+\lim_{S\to+\infty}S=+\infty,
   $$
   which diverges. Likewise, as a Lebesgue integral,
   $$
   \int_{\mathbb{R}}|g(x)|\,dx=\int_{\mathbb{R}}1\,dx=+\infty,
   $$
   so $g\notin L^{1}(\mathbb{R})$.

**Remark (non‑constant example).** If one prefers a non‑constant, decaying example, take
$$
h(x)=\frac{1}{1+|x|}.
$$
This is continuous on $\mathbb{R}$, integrable on every bounded interval (continuous on a compact set), yet
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}\frac{dx}{1+|x|}=2\int_{0}^{+\infty}\frac{dx}{1+x}=2\lim_{R\to+\infty}\ln(1+R)=+\infty,
$$
so $h\notin L^{1}(\mathbb{R})$ as well. The decay $h(x)\sim 1/|x|$ is too slow for summability.

**Conclusion.** Continuity on a bounded closed interval guarantees integrability there (a continuous function on a compact set is bounded and hence Riemann integrable). However, integrability on the unbounded domain $\mathbb{R}$ requires additionally that the function decay fast enough at infinity, a condition independent of continuity. The constant function $g\equiv 1$ — and more generally any continuous function that does not decay sufficiently rapidly — is continuous everywhere, integrable on every bounded interval, yet not integrable on $\mathbb{R}$.

Therefore, **yes**, there exists a function continuous everywhere but integrable only on bounded intervals.

